{"id":"reproductive-pharmacology","system":"Reproductive","subject":"Pharmacology","pages":[673,676],"rounds":[{"t":"GnRH analogs, estrogens, SERMs, aromatase inhibitors, and progestins","q":[{"s":"A man with metastatic prostate cancer starts continuous leuprolide. In the first week his bone pain worsens. Why?","o":["Testosterone falls immediately","Leuprolide is an aromatase inhibitor","Continuous GnRH agonists transiently stimulate LH and testosterone before downregulating receptors","Leuprolide blocks androgen receptors","Leuprolide directly stimulates tumor cells"],"e":["Wrong. Rises first.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. Tumor flare.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"673","k":"GnRH analogs"},{"s":"Leuprolide given in a pulsatile fashion acts as:","o":["A GnRH antagonist","An androgen receptor blocker","An aromatase inhibitor","A 5α-reductase inhibitor","A GnRH agonist, useful for infertility"],"e":["Wrong. Continuous use.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"673","k":"GnRH analogs"},{"s":"Which conditions are treated with continuous GnRH analogs?","o":["Hypogonadism and Kallmann syndrome","Osteoporosis","Uterine fibroids, endometriosis, precocious puberty and prostate cancer","Hypothyroidism","Breast-feeding suppression"],"e":["Wrong. Pulsatile.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"673","k":"GnRH analogs"},{"s":"Which drug lowers testosterone in prostate cancer without an initial flare?","o":["Goserelin","Flutamide","Finasteride","Degarelix","Leuprolide"],"e":["Wrong. Causes flare.","Wrong. Receptor blocker.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. GnRH antagonist.","Wrong. Causes flare."],"a":3,"p":"674","k":"Degarelix"},{"s":"A 38-year-old woman who smokes a pack a day asks for combined oral contraceptives. Why is this contraindicated?","o":["Risk of endometrial cancer","Risk of osteoporosis","Increased risk of cardiovascular events and th